From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Collins Ikechukwu <collinschristroa@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 14:02:45 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] scf: flag suspect traffic instead of counting it (bot
 layers 1 to 4)

Events that fail any check are stored in scf_flagged_event, apart from
website_event, so Umami's reports never count them while operators can
see who is being inflated and how. Each layer has its own switch:

1. Real browser (SCF_ENFORCE_ORIGIN): Origin matches the site's domains
   and Fetch Metadata headers are present. Replaces the Redis-only
   rejection counters.
2. Proof of JavaScript (SCF_POW_BITS): the tracker finds a nonce so that
   sha256(website:slot:url:nonce) starts with N zero bits, slot being the
   10-minute window, and sends it in x-lumenstat-pow. A small sync
   SHA-256 keeps the tracker dependency-free.
3. Data-center IPs (SCF_DATACENTER_RANGES): a CIDR file built from the
   cloud providers' published lists, matched in memory.
4. Many visitors per network (SCF_MAX_VISITORS_PER_IP_HOUR): distinct
   visitors per site, /24 or /48 and hour, counted in a Redis
   HyperLogLog under HMACs keyed by the daily salt. New visitors past the
   limit are flagged.

Flagged rows keep path, referrer domain, country and coarse client info,
never the IP or query string. GET /api/scf/flagged (admins only) returns
totals per site and reason, and the latest events for one site.

Also: COLLECT_API_ENDPOINT as a Docker build arg, and immutable cache
headers for versioned tracker releases under /v/.
